{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

The A/B Test widget draws the scorecard of
[`get_ab_test`](/docs/tools/get-ab-test): it computes nothing of its own. Its
headless twin, `ab_test_data`, is an alias of `get_ab_test` — same arguments,
same result — and the widget makes up to three calls to it:

| Tab | Call | Rows |
|---|---|---|
| **Scorecard** | `ab_test_data(diff, metrics, ...)` without `strata_col` and `period` | one per metric |
| **Strata** (when `strata_col` is set) | the same call with `strata_col` and `strata_pre_exposure` | one per metric × stratum |
| **Periods** (when `period` is set) | the same call with `period`, `period_basis` and `period_population` | one per metric × period |

Every other argument — `assignment`, `horizon`, `test`, `correction`,
`ci_level`, `path_col`, ... — applies to all three. Each tab therefore holds
exactly the rows `get_ab_test` returns for that call, so any number in the
widget can be reproduced, and quoted, from code.

The sidebar edits the arms, the metrics (the same editor as Segment Overview,
with a row label and an optional ratio-of-sums denominator), `ci_level`
(90 / 95 / 99 %), `correction`, `test`, the strata column and whether it was
fixed before exposure, `period` (day / week / month) and its basis, and
`horizon`. Every change recomputes. The `assignment` table is passed in code
only; a `state_file` keeps everything else, and the table must be passed again
when the widget is reopened.

### Reading the scorecard and the forest plot

Each scorecard row shows the two arms' values, the delta `group1 − group2` with
its interval, the relative delta, the adjusted p-value, the MDE and the verdict.
Below the table, a forest plot puts every delta on one line:

- **the dot** is the delta, **the whisker** its interval at `ci_level`, and
  **the vertical line** is 0. Metrics are in different units, so each row has
  its own scale, symmetric around 0: read *where the interval lies relative to
  0*, not how long it is next to another row's;
- **the colour is the verdict**: red for `increase`, blue for `decrease` (the
  library's diff colours: group 1 higher is red), grey for `inconclusive`;
  a **hollow** dot is `insufficient_data` — too little support for a test, the
  estimate shown descriptively;
- **units**: 0/1 metrics (`has_event`, `matches_pattern`, ...) as shares, their
  deltas in **percentage points**; totals, values and ratios in their own unit;
  `duration` and `time_between` in one time unit per row. Hover a row for the
  relative delta and its interval.

The interval is **per comparison and never adjusted**; the verdict reads the
**adjusted** p-value (`correction` within the rows of one metric). So a row can
have an interval that excludes 0 and still be `inconclusive` — trust the
verdict for the decision, and `correction="bonferroni"` when you need a
simultaneous interval (`delta_ci_low_adj` / `delta_ci_high_adj` in the
headless result).

**An inconclusive row is not "no effect".** Its interval says which effects the
data still allow; the MDE says which effect this design would have detected
with the requested power — a property of the design, not a bound on the true
effect. When the library computes a `reading` for each row, the scorecard shows
it in a **Reading** column (hover for the whole sentence, click to expand); it
spells out what is inconclusive, on what basis, and what would settle it.

A **support** badge flags rows the test still ran on but that need a second
look: `one arm only` (the behaviour exists in one arm — a loop the treatment
created) and `low support` (a bootstrap interval over few paths).

### Strata

One section per metric: a forest plot of the strata deltas and a table with the
stratum's share in each arm, its delta, the adjusted p-value and the
stratum-vs-rest p-value. The header carries two p-values that answer different
questions:

- **heterogeneity p** (Cochran's Q over the strata with enough support): do the
  strata deltas differ from each other? A low value is the evidence for "it
  works for some users and not for others"; strata deltas that merely look
  different are not.
- **balance p** (chi-square of the stratum × arm table): do the arms hold the
  same share of each stratum? A balanced stratum is not therefore a fair one —
  a stratum computed after exposure can be balanced and still selected by the
  treatment.

A stratum is a randomised comparison only if the treatment could not change who
is in it. Rows whose strata are not declared (`strata_pre_exposure=True`, or
"Declared pre-exposure" in the sidebar) or read from a truncation in the
recipe are **exploratory**: marked `*`, in italics, under a banner.

### Periods

A line through the period deltas over a band of their intervals, a dashed zero
line, and a table. Periods are intention-to-treat by default: a period compares
every path assigned before its end, active or not. A **hollow** point is a
period that is not complete — the data ends inside it, or the `horizon` cuts it
— and reads low for that reason alone. With `period_population="active"` the
rows compare only the paths active in the period: survivors, not randomised
groups; they are `descriptive`, carry no p-value and are drawn as **squares**.
The correction family is a metric's periods: a coloured period has survived
the correction across all of them, and every extra period costs power. Calendar
periods mix users at different times since their assignment; for an effect
that changes with exposure age (novelty), bin by
`period_basis="since_assignment"` (it needs an `assignment` table with
`assigned_at`), as the guide's
[periods section](/docs/ab-testing#periods-horizon-and-novelty) does.

### What the numbers assume

- **The path is the randomised unit.** Intervals treat paths as independent; a
  session grain for a per-user experiment gives intervals that are too narrow.
  With `assignment=`, a grain finer than the table's is refused.
- **The population.** Without `assignment`, the arms are the paths of the
  stream — users with at least one event, split by the `diff` segment. With an
  assignment table the comparison is intention-to-treat: every assigned path,
  silent ones counting as zeros, events before `assigned_at` dropped.
- **Metrics chosen before looking.** Metrics are separate correction families:
  with five metrics at 5 % each, one false "increase" somewhere is likely when
  nothing changed. Name the decision metric up front.
- **Strata fixed before exposure**, as above, and no filter on post-exposure
  behaviour (users who reached checkout) before the test.
- **The arms have the planned sizes.** Check with
  [`get_sample_ratio`](/docs/tools/get-sample-ratio) first; a sample ratio
  mismatch invalidates every number here.

The [A/B Testing guide](/docs/ab-testing) walks through all of this on the
bundled experiment, `rete.datasets.load_ab_test()`, with the numbers read out.

